energy cost of variation of a\nwavefunctional across field configurations. Quantum dynamics combines them, with the gauge constraints, to determine\nthe vacuum and the excitations~\\cite{KogutSusskind75,vanBaal00}. These are aspects of the same theory, not three extra\nforces. The same topology can carry different geometries and different spectra. I want to follow what changes in\nthe field, not only the number attached to that change. Van Baal describes how wavefunctionals spreading across\nidentified boundaries of configuration space become sensitive to its topology~\\cite{vanBaal92Topology}; that is a\nphysical connection between the global shape of the space and its low energies.\n\nA connection component at a point depends on the gauge; local invariant quantities such as $\\mathrm{tr}\\,F^2$ do not.\nLoops give another set of physical readings:\n$W_C=\\mathrm{tr}_\\rho\\,\\mathrm{Hol}_C(A)$ is unchanged by relabelling, because the trace does not see conjugation, and\nthe complete compatible loop data reconstruct a smooth connection up to gauge under the hypotheses of\nGiles' theorem~\\cite{Giles81}. A finite set of measured loops is not that complete data. Wilson's lattice theory~\\cite{Wilson74} is this\nbundle sampled: the base at the vertices of a grid of spacing $a$, a group element $U_\\ell$ on each edge, and the action\nread from the holonomy $U_p$ of each elementary square (plaquette).\n\n\\begin{claim}{PROVED}{The lattice measure lives on connections modulo gauge}\\label{b:wilson}\n$d\\mu_{a,L}=Z^{-1}\\exp\\bigl(-\\beta\\sum_p(1-\\tfrac12\\mathrm{tr}\\,U_p)\\bigr)\\prod_\\ell dU_\\ell$, $\\beta=4/g^2$, is gauge\ninvariant and descends to a probability measure on lattice connections modulo gauge. No gauge fixing is needed for\nthese integrals; this avoids, rather than solves, the Gribov gauge-fixing obstruction. Every Wilson loop is gauge invariant.\n\\evidence{Traces of holonomies and an invariant Haar measure~\\cite{Wilson74}; checked in my engine (\\S