AssertionError: 'Relation to earlier work' not found in "% s1: the problem, as it is posed. ASCII only.\n\\section*{1. The problem, as it is posed}\n\nThe target is not mine. It is the Millennium Prize problem as Jaffe and Witten wrote it~\\cite{JaffeWitten}. For every\ncompact simple gauge group $G$ (the symmetry group of the field, such as $SU(2)$ or $SU(3)$), construct quantum\nYang--Mills theory on four-dimensional space-time $\\mathbb R^4$ and show two things. First, it exists as a genuine\nquantum field theory: it satisfies axioms at least as strong as Wightman's~\\cite{StreaterWightman}, or in Euclidean form\nthose of Osterwalder and Schrader~\\cite{OS73,OS75} (Euclidean invariance, reflection positivity, regularity, clustering),\nfrom which a relativistic theory is reconstructed; it has local field operators for the gauge-invariant polynomials in\nthe curvature $F$, such as $\\mathrm{tr}\\,F^2$; at short distance it agrees with perturbation theory and asymptotic\nfreedom~\\cite{GrossWilczek73,Politzer73}; and it is non-trivial, not a free field in disguise. Second, it has a mass gap:\nthe spectrum above the vacuum begins at a strictly positive, finite energy. Normalized states\northogonal to the vacuum have energy expectation at least $\\Delta>0$; a superposition with a vacuum component need not.\n\nThe problem is hard because of two infinities. The \\emph{ultraviolet} is the fine grain: zoom in and the field has ever\nfiner ripples, infinitely many, which must be controlled together (renormalization). The \\emph{infrared} is the growing\nroom: zoom out, and the gap must survive in infinite space, exactly where the coupling becomes strong and no small\nparameter is left. Every computation, mine included, cuts off one or both, with a lattice of spacing $a$ or a room of\nfinite size. The difficulty is to remove the scaffolding and keep the answer.\n\nWhat has been done shows where the wall is. Interacting theories were built in two and three\ndimensions~\\cite{GlimmJaffe}; two-dimensional Yang--Mills is solved exactly~\\cite{Migdal75,GrossWitten80,Sengupta97,Levy03};\non a lattice at strong coupling the gap is a theorem~\\cite{OsterwalderSeiler78,ShenZhuZhu23}, at the wrong end of the\ncoupling. In four dimensions Ba{\\l}aban\ncontrolled the ultraviolet in a finite box~\\cite{Balaban}, and Magnen, Rivasseau and S\\'en\\'eor built the $SU(2)$\nSchwinger functions with an infrared cutoff kept~\\cite{MRS93}. Nobody has removed the infrared cutoff. Physically the\ngap is not in doubt: simulations find a lightest glueball, a scalar $0^{++}$~\\cite{MorningstarPeardon99}.\n\n\\begin{claim}{OPEN}{The Clay statement (my Main Conjecture)}\\label{b:clay}\nFor $G=SU(2)$, and then every compact simple $G$, quantum Yang--Mills theory on $\\mathbb R^4$ exists with every\nproperty above (OS and so Wightman axioms, local fields, short-distance agreement, non-triviality, a unique vacuum), and\nits Hamiltonian has nothing in $(0,\\Delta)$, with a nonempty excitation spectrum and finite first spectral threshold\n$m\\ge\\Delta=c\\,\\Lambda>0$. Here $\\Lambda$ is an energy scale in a fixed renormalization scheme,\nand $c>0$ a dimensionless number. I work at vacuum angle $\\theta=0$.\n\\evidence{The problem itself~\\cite{JaffeWitten}. My register splits it into 29 lines and maps every clause to them\n(Table~2); it rests on four hypotheses, H3, H4$'$, H6, H7~\\cite{ClosedSet}.}\n\\lines{1--29}\n\\end{claim}\n\nThe scale $\\Lambda$ is where the problem turns geometric. The classical equations contain no length; the quantum theory\nmakes an energy scale through the running coupling; in units $\\hbar=c=1$, $1/\\Lambda$ is the associated length.\nA gap that is a pure number times that energy scale is a\nproperty of shape, not of any box. So I begin with the shape.\n"

